# Yet another app package

Automating application bootstrapping
and best practices (according to us)

Rodrigo J. Fonseca
Software Engineer, Qive
rodrigo.fonseca@arquivei.com.br
https://linktr.ee/rodrigo.fonseca

## Context

At Qive, our Go applications typically run on Kubernetes.

- Package the application as a container image
- Configure the application through environment variables
- Monitor and alert with [Grafana](https://grafana.com/)
- Send structured JSON logs to [Loki](https://grafana.com/oss/loki/)

## The go-app package

- Configuration defaults, environment variables, and flags
- Prometheus metrics, readiness and liveness probes, and debug routes
- Structured logging with zerolog and slog support
- Memory overload signaling
- Graceful shutdown

## Dependencies

.code ../go.mod /^require \(/,/^\)/

- The configuration parser is vendored in `internal/thirdparty/uconfig`.
- The admin server uses Go's `net/http`.

## Configuration

Embed `app.Config` in your application's configuration struct.

.code ../examples/servefiles/main.go /^var \(/,/^\)/

`Bootstrap` applies defaults, environment variables, and flags, in that
order. For an explicit app instance, call `SetupConfig` to apply defaults and
overrides before passing the configuration to `New`.

## Inspect options and defaults

```sh
go run ./examples/servefiles/ -h
go run ./examples/servefiles/ -app-config-output=env
go run ./examples/servefiles/ -app-config-output=yaml
```

- Help prints usage and exits with status 1.
- `-app-adminserver-addr=:9000` (or `APP_ADMINSERVER_ADDR=:9000`) binds the admin server to all interfaces.
- `-http-addr=:8080` configures the servefiles example's separate HTTP server.

## Bootstrap and run

.code ../examples/quickstart/main.go /^func main/,/^}/

- `Bootstrap` loads configuration, sets up logging, and creates the default app.
- Initialize dependencies and register cleanup before calling `RunAndWait`.
- The built-in readiness check passes just before the main loop runs.

## Admin server

.code ../app.go /mux := http.NewServeMux/,/mux.Handle\("\/ready"/

- The listener defaults to `localhost:9000`.
- When enabled, it always registers `/metrics`, `/ready`, and `/healthy`.
- The `With.Metrics` and `With.Probes` settings currently have no effect on these routes.

## Readiness and liveness

.code ../example_test.go /^func ExampleProbeGroup_ServeHTTP/,/^}/

- Failed readiness can remove the workload from traffic.
- Failed liveness can trigger a restart according to the orchestrator's thresholds.

## Profiling and debugging

.code ../app.go /if c.App.AdminServer.With.DebugURLs/,/^\s*}/

- Debug routes are enabled by default. Disable them with `-app-adminserver-with-debugurls=false`.
- The routes include standard pprof profiles and dumps for goroutines, memory, and memory statistics.

```sh
curl http://localhost:9000/debug/pprof/heap > heap.pprof
```

## Graceful shutdown

1. The main loop returns or the process receives SIGINT or SIGTERM.
2. The built-in readiness check starts failing.
3. The app waits the configured grace period.
4. The app cancels the main-loop context and runs shutdown handlers.
5. After the shutdown attempt, the built-in liveness check starts failing.

Repeated calls to `RunAndWait` on the same app log a warning and return.

## Shutdown handlers

.code ../examples/shutdown-handlers/main.go /app\.RegisterShutdownHandler\(/,/^\s*}\)/

- Handlers run from highest to lowest priority.
- Timeouts use context cancellation. Handlers must observe it.
- `ErrorPolicyWarn` logs the error and continues. `ErrorPolicyAbort` reports the error to shutdown and later handlers may still run.
- `ErrorPolicyFatal` exits the process. `ErrorPolicyPanic` panics.
